\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $R$ be a regular local ring and $P,Q$ prime ideals for which $R/(P+Q)$ has finite length and $\dim(R/P)+\dim(R/Q)=\dim R$. Must Serre's intersection multiplicity
\[\chi^R(R/P,R/Q)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\operatorname{length}_R\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(R/P,R/Q)\]
be strictly positive?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-04203-8}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $R$ be a regular local ring and $P,Q$ prime ideals for which $R/(P+Q)$ has finite length and $\dim(R/P)+\dim(R/Q)=\dim R$. Must Serre's intersection multiplicity
\[\chi^R(R/P,R/Q)=\sum_{i\geq0}(-1)^i\operatorname{length}_R\operatorname{Tor}_i^R(R/P,R/Q)\]
be strictly positive?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1182 (ALG-022). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-04203-8}.
\end{itemize}
